</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span><span id="A3.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification performance of reasoning models across problem difficulty.</span> <math id="A3.F9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TPR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>TPR</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TPR}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TNR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>TNR</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TNR}</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen3-8B-Thinking and Qwen3-32B-Thinking across difficulty quartiles in three domains. <math id="A3.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TPR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>TPR</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TPR}</annotation></semantics></math> increases from hardest to easiest problems in all domains. <math id="A3.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{TNR}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>TNR</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{TNR}</annotation></semantics></math> also shows an upward trend with decreasing difficulty for both reasoning models across all three domains.</figcaption>
</figure>
